WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.256
In a game, look at a wood floor or a brick wall far away. If the texture isn't filtered, it sparkles. Patterns crawl and shimmer every time the camera moves.

00:00:10.606 --> 00:00:15.406
Turn on mipmaps and the far floor calms down. It goes a little soft instead.

00:00:15.756 --> 00:00:23.244
Most people say mipmaps blur things because they're far away. That's not really the reason, so let's look at what's going on.

00:00:24.444 --> 00:00:29.969
Here's the same room from the same camera. On the left, no mipmap. On the right, a mipmap.

00:00:30.319 --> 00:00:38.959
Without it, the wood grain sparkles, and the small letters on the wall turn into a moire pattern, false wavy lines that aren't in the texture.

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:42.851
With it, the wood and the letters go soft. Soft, but honest.

00:00:43.201 --> 00:00:50.603
Down a hallway it's the same story. The far tiles crawl on the left. The near doors look identical on both sides.

00:00:51.803 --> 00:00:59.953
The number that matters is the footprint, written rho. It's how many texture pixels, called texels, land inside one screen pixel.

00:01:00.303 --> 00:01:10.351
Up close, one texel covers several pixels. Far away, many texels crowd into one pixel. At rho of eight, eight texels across share a single pixel.

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:16.482
But each pixel only takes one sample. It's like trying to describe eight stripes with a single dot.

00:01:17.682 --> 00:01:26.237
There's a hard rule for sampling. A grid of pixels can only show patterns up to half a cycle per pixel. That limit is called Nyquist.

00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:33.627
Anything finer doesn't just disappear. It folds back and shows up as a slower, fake pattern. That's aliasing.

00:01:33.977 --> 00:01:44.729
Put simply, a texture frequency folds whenever it's above one over two times rho. At rho of eight, anything finer than one cycle every sixteen texels folds.

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:53.271
So the sparkle isn't noise. It's real fine detail, folded into fake patterns, because the pixel grid is too coarse to hold it.

00:01:54.471 --> 00:01:58.993
To see it cleanly, use a test pattern of rings that get finer toward the edge.

00:01:59.343 --> 00:02:04.762
Shrink it to rho of eight with plain sampling, and fake rings appear everywhere. That's pure moire.

00:02:05.112 --> 00:02:09.443
Smooth sampling between neighbours softens it, but the fake rings are still there.

00:02:09.793 --> 00:02:16.427
Now use a mipmap. The fake rings are gone. What's left is the part of the pattern a pixel can legally show.

00:02:17.627 --> 00:02:26.011
So a mipmap isn't a distance blur. It's a legal limit. It removes exactly the detail the pixel grid can't hold, before sampling.

00:02:26.361 --> 00:02:32.484
Each level is made by averaging two by two blocks of the level above. That averaging is the filter.

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:41.879
Skip the averaging and just pick every other texel, and you get a smaller picture with the same fake rings. A pyramid without a filter isn't a mipmap.

00:02:43.079 --> 00:02:48.754
We can measure it, by counting the energy in the fake patterns. The measure is called A C power.

00:02:49.104 --> 00:02:55.611
Smooth sampling cuts it by a bit more than half. The box filtered mipmap cuts it by about thirty times.

00:02:55.961 --> 00:03:00.505
The pyramid without the filter scores just as badly as no mipmap at all.

00:03:01.705 --> 00:03:07.763
One control. Up close, when one texel covers a pixel or more, mipmaps shouldn't change anything.

00:03:08.113 --> 00:03:14.492
And at rho of one, the measured difference is zero. The near doors in the hallway agree for the same reason.

00:03:14.842 --> 00:03:22.821
As rho climbs from one to sixteen, the folding takes over from rho of four onward, and only the mipmapped row stays clean.

00:03:24.021 --> 00:03:32.234
A few limits. This was a software renderer, so it says nothing about how real graphics cards pick levels, or how fast they are.

00:03:32.584 --> 00:03:37.107
The room pictures only show the effect. The numbers come from the test pattern.

00:03:37.457 --> 00:03:48.273
And the softness that's left on far floors is real. A round filter blurs the narrow side of a slanted footprint too much. That's the job of anisotropic filtering.

00:03:49.473 --> 00:03:57.494
A far texture packs in more detail than a pixel can hold. Without a filter, that detail folds into sparkle and crawl.

00:03:57.844 --> 00:04:04.393
A mipmap removes it first, by averaging. The blur you see is the legal limit, not a distance effect.

00:04:04.743 --> 00:04:08.029
That's why mipmaps are standard in real time graphics.

00:04:08.379 --> 00:04:14.416
For further reading, there's Williams on pyramidal parametrics, and Shannon and Nyquist on sampling.

